\description{
Runs Slicing Aided Hyper Inference (\code{\link[=image_detect_sahi]{image_detect_sahi()}}) on a batch of images
defined by a directory path, a filename pattern, a vector of file paths,
or a list of \code{Image} objects. Ultra-high-resolution images (such as drone orthophotos,
aerial mapping surveys, and macro scans) are dynamically sliced into overlapping tiles
of size \verb{slice_width x slice_height}, deep learning inference is executed per slice,
and all candidate boxes are transformed into global image coordinates and unified
using Non-Maximum Merging (Greedy NMM) or standard NMS.
Supports sequential or parallel processing (via \code{mirai}), optional RStudio Background Job execution,
saving annotated detection images to disk, and returns a consolidated predictions dataset
along with per-image and per-class summaries.
}
\section{Performance}{

The model is resolved once and every image reuses the cached ONNX session. With
\code{parallel = TRUE} each worker process loads its own copy of the model, so:
\itemize{
\item on CPU (\code{engine = "cpu"}) and \code{threads = 0}, each worker gets
\verb{physical cores / workers} ONNX threads to avoid oversubscribing the CPU;
\item on GPU (\code{engine = "gpu"}) concurrency is capped at
\code{getOption("pliman.gpu_workers", 2)}, because every worker opens its own GPU
session (GPU memory grows with the number of workers);
\item temporary workers are started and stopped on every call. Start persistent
workers with \code{\link[=pliman_daemons]{pliman_daemons()}} to keep models loaded across calls;
\item pass file paths rather than in-memory images, so only paths are sent to the
workers.
}
}

